# The howdy command

`howdy` is the command line for Howdy. It creates new projects, writes code
into the project you are working in, adds Howdy packages and wires them up,
checks the project, lists its routes, and runs the development server. It
is a single program written in Go, and
[Getting started](/docs/guides/getting-started) shows how to install it.

## Commands

- `howdy new <project_name>` creates a project, with a database, the dev
  admin, a console and guides for coding agents, or without whichever you
  leave out. See [Creating a project](/docs/cli/new).
- `howdy create ...` writes code in the shape of the project: a JSON API, a
  set of pages, a repo or a migration around a new table, and pages, live
  pages, components, live components, layouts and emails on their own. See
  [Generating code](/docs/cli/create).
- `howdy add ...` adds a Howdy package and wires it into the app, the dev
  server and the tests: `auth`, `mail`, `flags`, `openapi` and `remote`.
  `howdy add docker` adds the files to deploy in a container. See
  [Adding packages](/docs/cli/add).
- `howdy check` formats, builds and tests the project, and `howdy routes`
  lists every route it serves and where it is defined. See
  [Checking the project](/docs/cli/check).
- `howdy dev` runs the current project with hot reload.
- `howdy completion bash|zsh|fish|powershell` prints a completion script
  for your shell.

Every command explains itself with `--help`, including its arguments, flags
and examples:

```sh
howdy --help
howdy new --help
howdy create controller --help
```

`howdy --version` prints the version you have installed.

## Flags every command shares

The commands that write code, `new`, `create` and `add`, take the same
flags:

- `--dry-run` shows which files the command would create and modify, and
  what it would leave for you to do, without changing anything. Use it to
  see what a command does before letting it.
- `--json` prints the result as one JSON object instead of progress, for a
  script or a coding agent to read. See [Output for scripts and
  agents](#Output-for-scripts-and-agents).
- `--plain` prints progress as one line per step instead of animating,
  which reads better in CI logs.
- `--skip-deps`, for `new` and `add`, leaves out `gleam deps download`
  after `gleam.toml` changes, so you can run it yourself later.

`check` and `routes` take `--json` too.

## What it needs

The CLI writes Gleam code and then hands over to Gleam's own tools, so it
looks for `gleam` on your `PATH` before it writes anything, and stops with an
error if it is missing. It warns you if `erl` is missing too, because the
project will not run without Erlang.

Howdy's packages are downloaded from Hex, so you do not need a copy of
Howdy on your machine. A project made with `--source git` takes them from
GitHub instead, and then `howdy new` and `howdy add` also need *Git*
installed.

Every command but `new` runs inside a project. It looks for the nearest
directory with a `gleam.toml`, starting where you are and walking up, so it
works from any folder inside the project, and stops with an error outside
one rather than guessing.

## Running the project

```sh
howdy dev
```

`howdy dev` finds the project you are in and runs `gleam dev` there, which
rebuilds and refreshes the browser as you change the code, and serves the
[admin](/docs/admin/overview) at `/_howdy`. Output is streamed to your
terminal, and Ctrl-C stops the server.

## Output for scripts and agents

With `--json`, a command that writes code prints one object and nothing
else:

```json
{
  "command": "howdy create page blog about",
  "ok": true,
  "dry_run": false,
  "created": [
    "src/my_app/features/blog/blog_controller.gleam",
    "src/my_app/features/blog/pages/about_page.gleam",
    "test/my_app/features/blog/pages/about_page_test.gleam"
  ],
  "modified": ["src/my_app.gleam"],
  "manual_steps": [],
  "warnings": [],
  "next": [],
  "notes": [
    "The new blog controller is registered in src/my_app.gleam.",
    "Add the page's content in src/my_app/features/blog/pages/about_page.gleam."
  ]
}
```

- `created` and `modified` list the files, relative to the project.
- `manual_steps` is what the command could not wire up itself, such as a
  registration in a file that no longer has the shape `howdy new` gave it.
  An empty list means nothing is left to do.
- `warnings` is anything to know about, such as a missing tool.
- `next` and `notes` say what to do and where things went.

A command that fails prints `"ok": false` with an `error`, and exits with
status 1.

A project made by `howdy new` comes with `AGENTS.md`, which tells a coding
agent to use these commands rather than write the same code by hand, to run
`howdy check` after every change, and where things go. `CLAUDE.md` imports
it for Claude Code, and `.claude/skills/` holds guides for common tasks,
which `howdy add` extends with a guide for each package it adds.
